{
  "dom_id": "step-q350910",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226182/assets/87481d3de1bd3fb02a2fbaa344f120421052335624e8d42b6dd853382b9f764e.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226182/assets/bd558d08aa48a17d04b9c3929a5b49a21e5fd7bde8ebfe4a07f8cabfd824b981.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226182/assets/b8c8e0216a8ee045a9d7a570bc26d1765fc54e2110b1c1d2fbbc8f59d1ec22ef.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt{x})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\sqrt{x}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226182/assets/4cd4e1fb0ad340da9a2e146fa261c6a50e4578f1b77564c8d6a5c6a2f27c81f9.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,({x}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\sqrt{x}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(\\sqrt{x}+1)}^{2}-{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{4}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{5}(1)^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{29\\pi }{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
